  \uc0\u9474 \
  \uc0\u9660 \
force on moving charges\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Electromagnetism starts looking less like a list of physics facts and more like a 
\f1\b dynamical system
\f0\b0 .\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 2. The Model \'97 what actually interacts with what?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 We can compress most introductory electromagnetism into four entities:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 CHARGE \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  FIELD \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  FORCE \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  MOTION\
   \uc0\u9650                                   \u9474 \
   \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9496 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Why the loop?\
Because:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 charge\
  \uc0\u8595  creates\
field\
  \uc0\u8595  produces\
force\
  \uc0\u8595  changes\
motion of charge\
  \uc0\u8595  changes\
field\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 That\'92s the conceptual engine.\
Imagine two electrons.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 electron A (-)                         electron B (-)\
\
     \uc0\u9679   \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  creates E field \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9658  \u9679 \
                                             \uc0\u9474 \
                                             \uc0\u9660 \
                                           force\
                                             \uc0\u9474 \
                                             \uc0\u9660 \
                                        B accelerates\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 But B isn\'92t passive.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 electron A (-)                         electron B (-)\
\
     \uc0\u9679  \u9668 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  fields \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  \u9679 \
     \uc0\u9474                                        \u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660                                        \u9660 \
   force                                   force\
     \uc0\u9474                                        \u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660                                        \u9660 \
   motion                                  motion\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Each charge participates in the electromagnetic field and responds to it.\
Already we have a usable model:\

\f1\b Matter changes fields; fields change matter.
\f0\b0 \
Keep that sentence. It\'92s one of the load-bearing ideas.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 3. Electricity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Start with stationary charge.\
An electron produces an electric field around itself.\
Conceptually:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0               \uc0\u8593 \
           \uc0\u8598      \u8599 \
\
        \uc0\u8592     (-)    \u8594 \
\
           \uc0\u8601      \u8600 \
              \uc0\u8595 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 For a negative charge, the field direction points inward; for a positive charge, outward.\
Put another charge in that field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 charge A\
   \uc0\u9474 \
   \uc0\u9660 \
creates electric field\
   \uc0\u9474 \
   \uc0\u9660 \
charge B experiences field\
   \uc0\u9474 \
   \uc0\u9660 \
force\
   \uc0\u9474 \
   \uc0\u9660 \
acceleration\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 The familiar statement:\
Opposite charges attract; like charges repel.\
is therefore the 
\f1\b observable behavior
\f0\b0  of the deeper model.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 4. Now move the charges\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is where electricity starts turning into magnetism.\
Suppose electrons move through a wire:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 WIRE\
\
e\uc0\u8315  \u8594  e\u8315  \u8594  e\u8315  \u8594  e\u8315  \u8594  e\u8315 \
\uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \
\
       electric current\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Moving charge = 
\f1\b electric current
\f0\b0 .\
But moving charges produce something else:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0               magnetic field\
                 \uc0\u8634  \u8634  \u8634 \
                    \uc0\u9474 \
                    \uc0\u9474 \
current \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9658 \
                    \uc0\u9474 \
                 \uc0\u8635  \u8635  \u8635 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 So:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 CHARGE\
  \uc0\u9474 \
  \uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  stationary \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9658  electric field\
  \uc0\u9474 \
  \uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  moving \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9658  electric + magnetic effects\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 This is our first major conceptual bridge.\
Electricity and magnetism aren\'92t arbitrary phenomena someone decided to put in the same chapter.\
They\'92re structurally connected.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 5. Now introduce change\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Things get much more interesting when fields 
\f1\b change over time
\f0\b0 .\
A changing magnetic field generates an electric field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 changing B\
    \uc0\u9474 \
    \uc0\u9660 \
electric field\
    \uc0\u9474 \
    \uc0\u9660 \
force on electrons\
    \uc0\u9474 \
    \uc0\u9660 \
current\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 This is electromagnetic induction.\
And this seemingly abstract relationship gives us:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 mechanical motion\
       \uc0\u8595 \
changing magnetic field\
       \uc0\u8595 \
electric field\
       \uc0\u8595 \
electron movement\
       \uc0\u8595 \
electric current\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Which means:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 generator\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs26 \cf0 ROTATION \uc0\u8594  MAGNETIC CHANGE \u8594  ELECTRICITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Reverse the relationship:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 ELECTRICITY \uc0\u8594  MAGNETIC FIELD \u8594  FORCE \u8594  ROTATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 motor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Same system.\
Different direction of energy conversion.\
That\'92s an example of why understanding relationships beats memorizing definitions.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 6. Then comes the beautiful part\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 A changing magnetic field creates an electric field.\
But a changing electric field also creates a magnetic field.\
So imagine creating a disturbance:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 changing\
electric field\
     \uc0\u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660 \
changing\
magnetic field\
     \uc0\u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660 \
changing\
electric field\
     \uc0\u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660 \
changing\
magnetic field\
     \uc0\u9474 \
     \uc0\u9660 \
    ...\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 The disturbance can sustain itself and propagate.\
Schematically:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0         Electric field\
             \uc0\u8593 \
             \uc0\u9474        /\\\
             \uc0\u9474       /  \\\
\uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9532 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 /\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \\\u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9472 \u9658  propagation\
             \uc0\u9474     /      \\\
             \uc0\u9474 \
\
             \uc0\u10178 \
\
        Magnetic field\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 That\'92s an 
\f1\b electromagnetic wave
\f0\b0 .\
And its propagation speed in vacuum is:\

\f1\b c \uc0\u8776  299,792,458 m/s.
\f0\b0 \
Which happens to be\'85\
the speed of light.\
This wasn\'92t merely a coincidence discovered afterward. Maxwell\'92s electromagnetic theory implied waves propagating at a speed corresponding to the known speed of light.\
And suddenly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 ELECTRICITY\
      \\\
       \\\
        \uc0\u9472 \u9472 \u9472  ELECTROMAGNETISM \u9472 \u9472 \u9472  LIGHT\
       /\
      /\
MAGNETISM\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 became one phenomenon.\
That was one of the great unifications in physics.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 7. And \'93light\'94 is much bigger than visible light\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

